ntf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}", "option_a": "10 20", "option_b": "20 10", "option_c": "0 0", "option_d": "20 20", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "This is the XOR swap algorithm. It swaps two variables without requiring temporary storage: x becomes 20, y becomes 10.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "code_snippet": "#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    x = x ^ y;\n    y = x ^ y;\n    x = x ^ y;\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}", "language": "c"}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "In C, what is the output of printf(\"%d\", sizeof('A')); on a 32-bit/64-bit compiler?", "option_a": "1", "option_b": "2", "option_c": "4", "option_d": "Undefined", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "In standard C (unlike C++), character literals like 'A' have type 'int', so sizeof('A') == sizeof(int) == 4 bytes.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 35, "code_snippet": null, "language": null}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 15, "question_text": "In C++, what is a 'pure virtual function'?", "option_a": "A function declared with static linkage", "option_b": "A virtual function assigned = 0 that must be overridden by non-abstract derived classes", "option_c": "A function with an empty body {}", "option_d": "A virtual constructor", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "A pure virtual function is declared as 'virtual void func() = 0;' and makes the enclosing class abstract.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "code_snippet": null, "language": null}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 15, "question_text": "What will be the output of this C++ program?\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 10;\n    int &ref = a;\n    ref = 20;\n    cout << a;\n    return 0;\n}", "option_a": "10", "option_b": "20", "option_c": "Address of a", "option_d": "Compilation Error", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "In C++, a reference is an alias to the original variable. Modifying 'ref' directly modifies 'a', so a becomes 20.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "code_snippet": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int a = 10;\n    int &ref = a;\n    ref = 20;\n    cout << a;\n    return 0;\n}", "language": "cpp"}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 16, "question_text": "In Java, what is the main purpose of the 'finally' block in exception handling?", "option_a": "To execute clean-up code regardless of whether an exception was thrown or caught", "option_b": "To rethrow the caught exception to caller", "option_c": "To execute only when an exception occurs", "option_d": "To terminate the JVM process", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "A 'finally' block always executes upon exiting the try-catch structure, ensuring resources like file handles and DB sockets are closed.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "code_snippet": null, "language": null}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 16, "question_text": "Which string class in Java is thread-safe due to synchronized methods?", "option_a": "StringBuilder", "option_b": "StringBuffer", "option_c": "StringTokenizer", "option_d": "StringFormatter", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "StringBuffer is thread-safe because its methods are synchronized, whereas StringBuilder is unsynchronized for higher single-threaded speed.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "code_snippet": null, "language": null}, {"company_name": "Hexaware", "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ", "subtopic_id": 19, "question_text": "What is the output of the following Python slice operation: 'Hexaware'[::-1]?", "option_a": "'Hexaware'", "option_b": "'erawaxeH'", "option_c": "'erawahxeH'", "option_d": "'H'", "correct_option": "B", "explanation":